title: Pausing the watcher
tags: [[Watcher]]
kind: guide
summary: mf watch pause stops the flush in progress and keeps the next from starting; the events stay buffered until mf watch resume.

A flush is not always short: a directory of a hundred thousand files dropped into a
watched repository is one batch, and applying it holds the repository for as long as it
takes. To say ''stop'':

```
mf -n photos watch pause      # stop the flush in progress; buffer what comes
mf -n photos watch            # paused  12043 event(s) waiting  …
mf -n photos watch resume     # apply what accumulated (after the quiet period)
```

Stopping a flush — `mf watch pause`, the ''Stop'' of its task in the repos panel, or
`mf task show <id> --stop` — means ''pause ingestion'' for this repository:

* the flush stops at the next event; the group in progress is ''abandoned, not committed'' — no half-group is ever visible — and groups already committed stay;
* the batch goes ''back to the head of the buffer'': on resume it is applied again from its start (the event semantics are idempotent for that);
* the repository then ''stays paused'': the watcher keeps buffering, the executor does not flush. Without the pause, the next event would restart the very flush just stopped. A stopped flush is not a failed one.

The repos panel shows `tracking paused — N event(s) waiting` in amber, with a
''Resume'' button (`repos:resume`); a pause from the CLI shows there too. A paused
repository is otherwise fully usable — queries, writes, reconcile (which reads the disk
directly, so it picks up what the buffer carries) and undo all work.

The pause is in memory: unloading the repository or restarting the daemon clears it —
and the buffered events with it, so run a reconcile afterwards. It is "let me look
before it lands", not a setting: the durable way to stop tracking a subtree is
`mf_watch = false` ([[Tracking a folder]]). Pausing and resuming are idempotent.
